  {
    q: 'Which port should I use when shipping from China to the UK?',
    a: 'Use Felixstowe (UN/LOCODE GBFXT) as the UK seaport destination — it is the UK\u2019s busiest container port, with a published throughput of ≈ 3.6m TEU (2023), a max draft of ≈ 16 m, and key terminals Trinity Terminal and Dooley Terminal. A direct China-to-UK call is often simpler than a Rotterdam discharge plus overland relay, because the latter means clearing the EU first and then the UK again.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I ship China-to-UK by rail?',
    a: 'Yes, but with a Channel crossing. New Silk Road trains from Xi\u2019an, Chengdu and Chongqing reach continental termini in roughly 18–22 days, and the UK leg adds the Channel crossing — a tunnel or ferry handover that pushes UK rail transit to roughly 18–25 days. Rail costs roughly two to three times more per container than sea, so price the financing cost of the extra sea days against the rail premium before defaulting to ocean.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need a GB EORI to import into the UK?',
    a: 'Yes. A GB EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) number — starting with "GB" — is required for UK import clearance, and it must be registered before your goods arrive. It is separate from an EU EORI, which does not work for UK imports. It is the single most common post-Brexit stumbling block for China-to-UK importers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the UK still part of the EU customs union?',
    a: 'No. Since 2021 the UK has its own customs regime — the UK Global Tariff, a separate GB EORI, and its own declarations. EU procedures no longer apply to UK imports, so you cannot clear a China shipment through an EU member state and treat it as already imported into the UK.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the VAT rate in the UK?',
    a: 'The UK applies a 20% standard VAT rate, with a 5% reduced rate and 0% on some items. Import VAT is charged on the duty-inclusive value, so the effective tax is slightly above the headline 20% — budget the compound (customs value + duty) × 1.20 rather than a flat 20% on the customs value.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FCL and LCL for China to the UK?',
    a: 'FCL gives you exclusive use of a 20GP, 40GP or 40HQ and is usually the fit for large or heavy loads, priced per container. LCL consolidates your cargo with other importers and is priced per cubic metre. A common planning split is around 15 CBM — below that LCL is usually cheaper, above it FCL starts to win — but that is a LOW-confidence rule of thumb, so request quotes on both sides of the split before deciding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the £135 de-minimis threshold and how does it work?',
    a: 'Imports below £135 are charged import VAT at the point of sale (the seller registers and accounts for it), while goods above £135 are assessed at the border. Duty applies at lower thresholds depending on value. Confirm the current rules with HMRC before relying on these figures, as the mechanism depends on whether the seller or the importer accounts for the VAT.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for UK customs clearance?',
    a: 'The standard set is a commercial invoice, bill of lading (or rail consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct UK-specific HS classification, backed by your GB EORI number. Regulated goods also need UKCA marking evidence (with CE still accepted for many goods during transition) — confirm the exact document set for your product class with a licensed UK broker.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does CE marking still apply in the UK, or do I need UKCA?',
    a: 'The UK introduced UKCA as its own conformity mark to replace CE. CE remains accepted for many goods during transition, but regulated products increasingly require UKCA — confirm the current status for your specific product class rather than assuming CE alone will clear.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need SABER or SASO to import into the UK?',
    a: 'No. SABER and SASO are Saudi-only conformity systems and do not apply to the UK. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a UK quote. The UK uses UKCA marking (with CE accepted for many goods during transition) instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with HMRC or your broker.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do demurrage and detention differ in the UK?',
    a: 'Demurrage is the charge the terminal applies when import cargo stays in the port beyond the allowed free time after discharge. Detention is the charge the ocean carrier (or rail operator) applies when the container is kept beyond the equipment free time after collection. They are two separate clocks with two separate payees. UK free-time periods and per-day rates are not published in the verified snapshot — confirm both allowances in writing before booking and file the UK declaration promptly so neither clock runs.',
  },
